WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.639
Reinforcement learning promises optimal decisions,

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:07.951
but almost every algorithm quietly assumes the
world never changes.

00:00:08.031 --> 00:00:12.264
In real settings like mobile health, traffic
control, and robotics,

00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:18.058
that stationarity assumption breaks down over time
and quietly poisons the learned policy.

00:00:18.138 --> 00:00:23.529
This paper introduces a doubly robust statistical
test that checks whether an offline

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:26.555
Markov decision process is actually stationary,

00:00:26.635 --> 00:00:28.550
and pinpoints where it changes.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:33.764
The test pairs modern machine learning estimators
with semiparametric statistics,

00:00:33.844 --> 00:00:40.008
so it controls false alarms while staying powerful
even when the state space is high dimensional.

00:00:40.088 --> 00:00:43.549
Across four simulations and a real intern health study,

00:00:43.629 --> 00:00:51.080
it detects change points others miss and lets
policies recover the reward that non stationarity would otherwise cost.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.862
Reinforcement learning agents are trained to find
the optimal policy,

00:00:55.942 --> 00:01:02.032
but nearly every algorithm leans on one fragile
assumption: that the environment never changes.

00:01:02.112 --> 00:01:04.371
This is the stationarity assumption,

00:01:04.451 --> 00:01:09.307
requiring the state transition and reward functions
to stay fixed over time.

00:01:09.387 --> 00:01:11.710
In the real world, that rarely holds.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:16.452
Robotics, healthcare, and digital marketing all
drift over long horizons,

00:01:16.532 --> 00:01:21.388
and a policy learned as if the world were frozen
quietly becomes suboptimal,

00:01:21.468 --> 00:01:22.882
sometimes even harmful.

00:01:22.962 --> 00:01:28.663
The problem this paper tackles is how to reliably
tell whether an offline decision-making

00:01:28.743 --> 00:01:33.210
system is actually stationary before you trust the
policy it produced.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.117
Why does this matter now?

00:01:35.197 --> 00:01:37.238
Consider the Intern Health Study,

00:01:37.318 --> 00:01:44.757
a year-long mobile-health trial that nudges
first-year physicians toward healthier habits through push notifications.

00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:49.898
The effect of those nudges wanes over time, a
textbook case of non-stationarity.

00:01:49.978 --> 00:01:53.048
Similar drift shows up in traffic signal control,

00:01:53.128 --> 00:01:56.775
where flow patterns swing between peak and off-peak hours.

00:01:56.855 --> 00:02:03.587
Ignoring these shifts leads to policies that send
prompts at the wrong moments and erode long-term reward.

00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:07.893
Prior stationarity tests either demand knowledge of
the true model,

00:02:07.973 --> 00:02:12.456
or fall back on linear approximations that collapse
in high dimensions,

00:02:12.536 --> 00:02:15.220
leaving a real gap for modern applications.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.352
The paper's core contribution is a model-based,

00:02:18.432 --> 00:02:23.656
doubly robust procedure that tests the stationarity
assumption and locates change points

00:02:23.736 --> 00:02:25.705
in offline reinforcement learning.

00:02:25.785 --> 00:02:31.068
Doubly robust means the test controls the type-one
error as long as either the transition

00:02:31.148 --> 00:02:35.528
function or the marginal state-action distribution
is correctly specified,

00:02:35.608 --> 00:02:39.445
so you get valid inference even when one nuisance
model is wrong.

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:40.711
On top of the method,

00:02:40.791 --> 00:02:46.496
the authors prove size control and double
robustness under a bidirectional asymptotic framework,

00:02:46.576 --> 00:02:51.920
where either the number of trajectories or the
length of the horizon may grow to infinity,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.440
and they supply a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap to
compute honest p-values.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.465
Here is how the test works.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.445
At each candidate change point,

00:03:00.525 --> 00:03:06.832
it compares the pooled transition dynamics before
and after that time using a CUSUM-style statistic.

00:03:06.912 --> 00:03:12.900
Naively plugging modern machine-learning estimators
into this comparison introduces heavy bias,

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.030
so the authors add a mean-zero augmentation term,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.969
producing a doubly robust estimating function.

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:24.654
That lets flexible models like neural networks and
random forests estimate the transition

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:28.614
and state-action distributions without spoiling the
inference.

00:03:28.694 --> 00:03:35.129
Sample splitting and cross-fitting normalize the
statistic across candidate points and test functions,

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:41.389
and a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap approximates
its null distribution to yield the final p-value.

00:03:41.469 --> 00:03:45.157
The maximum over time and test functions of the
normalized,

00:03:45.237 --> 00:03:48.990
CUSUM-weighted statistic gives the test statistic
gamma-hat.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.852
The method is stress-tested across four numerical
studies and one real dataset.

00:03:53.932 --> 00:03:58.414
A discrete-state toy example illustrates the double
robustness property directly.

00:03:58.494 --> 00:04:02.977
High-dimensional synthetic data pushes the state
dimension from one up to thirty and pits

00:04:03.057 --> 00:04:07.539
the test against two existing baselines,

00:04:07.619 --> 00:04:12.101
ODCP and CUSUM-RL.

00:04:12.181 --> 00:04:16.663
A four-by-four grid world shows how detecting the
change point improves policy learning,

00:04:16.743 --> 00:04:21.226
and a batch-online semi-synthetic study mimics the
structure of the real trial.

00:04:21.306 --> 00:04:25.788
Finally, the authors apply the test to the Intern
Health Study,

00:04:25.868 --> 00:04:30.350
a twenty-one-week mobile-health micro-randomized
trial of medical interns in the United States.

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.533
The headline finding is about robustness to
dimensionality.

00:04:34.613 --> 00:04:38.416
In every setting, the proposed test keeps the
type-one error at the nominal level,

00:04:38.496 --> 00:04:42.299
and crucially it still pinpoints the true change
point even when the state has ten,

00:04:42.379 --> 00:04:46.182
twenty, or thirty dimensions.

00:04:46.262 --> 00:04:50.065
By contrast,

00:04:50.145 --> 00:04:53.948
the CUSUM-RL baseline only recovers the change
point when the state is one-dimensional,

00:04:54.028 --> 00:04:57.831
and the ODCP method fails to control the type-one
error at all in high dimensions.

00:04:57.911 --> 00:05:01.714
The toy example confirms the double robustness in
action: size and power hold as long

00:05:01.794 --> 00:05:05.597
as at least one of the two nuisance models is right,

00:05:05.677 --> 00:05:09.480
with the strongest power when both are correct.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:15.240
The most informative ablation varies the model
misspecification level from mild to severe,

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.243
across five hundred replications.

00:05:17.323 --> 00:05:22.400
It shows the empirical size staying pinned at the
nominal level whenever at least one

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.737
of the two nuisance models remains correctly specified,

00:05:25.817 --> 00:05:29.134
a clean demonstration of the double robustness property.

00:05:29.214 --> 00:05:32.956
A second sweep varies kappa, the length of the
tested interval,

00:05:33.036 --> 00:05:38.235
to actually locate the single change point: the
test correctly holds its size while the

00:05:38.315 --> 00:05:44.120
null is true and its power climbs once the interval
crosses the true change point at twenty-five.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.442
A few numbers anchor the impact.

00:05:46.522 --> 00:05:52.447
The test sustains correct change-point detection
all the way up to a state dimension of thirty,

00:05:52.527 --> 00:05:56.175
whereas the closest competitor works only at
dimension one.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:06:03.507
It maintains the nominal five-percent significance
level across all four simulations and both real-data specialties.

00:06:03.587 --> 00:06:06.098
And on the real Intern Health Study data,

00:06:06.178 --> 00:06:11.028
it flags a genuine change point for Internal
Medicine interns at week sixteen,

00:06:11.108 --> 00:06:15.073
while correctly finding no change for the Family
Practice group.

00:06:15.153 --> 00:06:21.140
These results come from thousands of bootstrap
samples and hundreds of replications per setting.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:22.892
The takeaway is simple.

00:06:22.972 --> 00:06:29.144
By marrying the flexibility of modern machine
learning with the rigor of semiparametric statistics,

00:06:29.224 --> 00:06:35.142
this doubly robust CUSUM test reliably flags when
an offline reinforcement-learning environment

00:06:35.222 --> 00:06:36.595
stops being stationary,

00:06:36.675 --> 00:06:41.267
even in high dimensions, so that policies can be
relearned on the correct,

00:06:41.347 --> 00:06:42.972
stationary segment of data.

00:06:43.052 --> 00:06:45.056
When some homogeneity is present,

00:06:45.136 --> 00:06:50.486
detecting and adapting to change points recovers
near-oracle reward that stationary or

00:06:50.566 --> 00:06:53.770
sliding-window strategies simply leave on the table.
